{
  "esm": "1.2.0",
  "metadata": {
    "name": "MosaicMtemActivities",
    "description": "MOSAIC's activity coefficients, ion molalities and electrolyte activities for ONE size bin: SUBROUTINE compute_activities of chem/module_mosaic_therm.F (WRF v4.8.0, :8925-9531), with calculate_xt (:9822). It is what every MOSAIC equilibrium and flux routine (MESA, ASTEM) calls to know how far the liquid phase is from equilibrium with the gas, so it sits at the bottom of the semi-volatile partitioning.\n\nINPUTS a consumer supplies: the bin's liquid water W (aerosol_water_zsr.esm, the first thing compute_activities computes), the liquid-phase ion amounts and electrolyte amounts (nmol per m3 of air), the binary MTEM log-activity matrix at the cell's relative humidity (mtem_binary_log_gamma.esm), and the two equilibrium constants Keq_ll(1) (HSO4- = H+ + SO4--) and Keq_ll(3) (water's ion product), which update_thermodynamic_constants sets per cell from temperature.\n\nTHE REGIME. MOSAIC splits on the ratio of the cations NH4+ + Na+ + 2Ca++ to the sulfate (calculate_xt, X_T = (NH4 + Na + 2Ca)/(SO4 + MSA/2), or -1 with no sulfate or MSA). X_T > 2, or X_T < 0, is SULFATE-POOR: every electrolyte is fully dissociated and bisulfate is absent; otherwise SULFATE-RICH: bisulfate forms and the free H+ comes from a quadratic.\n\nTHE MIXING RULE (MTEM). log10 gamma_A = sum_E x_E log10 gamma_{A(E)}, a weighted mean of A's binary values over the branch's electrolytes E, weighted by their ionic mole fractions x_E = nu_E E_E / sum_F nu_F E_F (nu = ions per formula unit). The sulfate-poor branch mixes over ten electrolytes, the sulfate-rich over nine (nu_poor, nu_rich); which coefficients each branch forms this way, and which it sets to 1, is in mix_rich and nu_poor. With no electrolyte at all the Fortran sets every coefficient to 1.\n\nH+, SULFATE-POOR. With a_c = (2 SO4 + NO3 + Cl + MSA) - (2 Ca + NH4 + Na) the anion excess (molalities), charge balance with water's ion product K_w gives H+ (H+ - a_c) = K_w, i.e. H+ = (a_c + sqrt(a_c^2 + 4 K_w))/2. THE FORTRAN WRITES `mc(jc_h,ibin) = 0.5*a_c + sqrt(a_c**2 + 4.*Keq_ll(3))` -- the square root is NOT halved -- and this component reproduces it as written (FORTRAN_BUGS B27): it doubles H+ at neutrality, gives 1.5 a_c instead of a_c with an anion excess, and |a_c|/2 instead of K_w/|a_c| with a cation excess. H+ enters only the HNO3, HCl (and MSA) activities, which drive their gas-particle exchange.\n\nH+, SULFATE-RICH. With K = Keq_ll(1) gamma_{H.HSO4}^2 / gamma_{H2SO4}^3 and the charge balance c = NH4 + Na + 2Ca - NO3 - Cl - SO4_total - MSA (Ca++ is set to zero here: 'aqueous ca never exists in sulfate rich cases'), H+ solves H^2 + (K + c) H + K(c - SO4_total) = 0, taken from its positive root; where |4ac/b^2| < 1e-6 the Fortran uses the series sqrt(1 - x) expansion instead, with its own sign fix, and H+ is floored at 1e-7. Then SO4-- = SO4_total K/(H + K) and HSO4- = SO4_total - SO4--. No iteration: the coefficients that set K are computed before H+, from the mixing rule alone.\n\nACTIVITIES. activity_E = gamma_E^nu_E times the product of E's ion molalities, each to its count in E's formula (stoich, e.g. NH4NO3: NH4 NO3 gamma^2; letovicite: NH4^3 HSO4 SO4 gamma^5). Each branch forms some of them and sets others to zero (act_poor, act_rich).\n\nWHAT THE FORTRAN LEAVES STALE, and so what the tests can pin. compute_activities only assigns some entries of gam and activity on each path; the rest keep whatever an earlier call left. Sulfate-poor: the eight salts' coefficients and activities are set only where that salt's mole fraction is positive, and with no electrolyte at all the activities of the mixed set are skipped entirely; H2SO4, MSA, HHSO4, CaSO4 and CaCO3 coefficients are never set; and gam_ratio = gamma_NH4NO3^2/gamma_HNO3^2 then reads a possibly stale gamma_NH4NO3. Sulfate-rich: the MSA coefficient is never set although activity(MSA) reads it. This component computes a value for every entry; the tests assert only the entries the Fortran sets on that call. A zero water makes the Fortran return before computing anything; the component is not defined there.\n\nPRECISION. Double-precision arithmetic, with the default-real literals at their binary32 roundings (mosaic_thermo_parameters.esm): the 1.e-9 of the molalities and the 1.e-6 of the series switch. The Fortran sums the mixing rule in its own listing order and raises gamma to integer powers by multiplication; this component sums in index order and uses ^, which differ by rounding only.\n\nTESTS AND COVERAGE. Eleven calls from the chem170 compute_activities capture (4 sulfate-poor, 7 sulfate-rich), against the in-model double-precision values at rel 1e-9; measured 5.8e-15 worst over all 11068 recorded calls, both branches. Mutation-checked: halving the square root (i.e. FIXING B27) fails 12 assertions, the H+, HNO3 and HCl activities of the four sulfate-poor cases; dropping gamma^nu fails 69; the printed decimal 1e-9 in place of its binary32 value fails 129. The column never reaches X_T between 1.144 and 4.149, dissolved calcium, the series root or the 1e-7 floor of the sulfate-rich quadratic, a bin with no electrolyte, a sulfate-free bin, or a sulfate-poor bin away from charge balance, so 23 hand-specified bins were added on 2026-11-12 (kernels/mosaic_therm_driver (WRF fork earthsciml-instrumented-chem170) runs WRF's own compiled thermodynamics (main/libwrflib.a, the model's flags) on hand-specified bins, cases in data/eqwefic/chem170_scm/therm64/make_cases.py). Mode 1 runs conform_electrolytes, do_full_deliquescence and compute_activities; mode 3 then adds an ion excess the electrolytes do not carry, as ASTEM's flux updates leave between conform calls, which is the only way to reach a_c /= 0 or the series root, since conform_electrolytes snaps a nearly neutral bin to X_T = 2 exactly. Now covered: X_T = 1.5, 1.9, 1.995-1.9995, 2 (H+ floor), 2.5, 3, 3.3 and 6; Ca(NO3)2 and CaCl2 in the liquid; X_T = -1; a kappa-only bin with every coefficient 1; an anion excess (a_c > 0, where B27 gives 1.5 a_c) and a cation excess (a_c < 0, where it gives |a_c|/2). Mutation-checked: moving the regime split to 1.5 fails 190 assertions, dropping the H+ floor fails 12, and FIXING B27 now fails 31. STILL not caught: (i) the series root -- three bins take it (|x| < 1e-6, X_T = 1.9999999 and 1.999999), but there the closed-form root's cancellation error is about 1e-10 relative, under the 1e-9 tolerance, so routing them through the closed form changes no assertion; (ii) Ca++ in the sulfate-rich branch, which conform_electrolytes cannot produce (it binds calcium to sulfate first), so keeping it changes nothing.",
    "authors": [
      "EqWeFiC"
    ],
    "references": [
      {
        "citation": "Zaveri, R.A., Easter, R.C., Wexler, A.S. (2005). A new method for multicomponent activity coefficients of electrolytes in aqueous atmospheric aerosols. J. Geophys. Res. 110, D02201."
      },
      {
        "citation": "Zaveri, R.A., Easter, R.C., Fast, J.D., Peters, L.K. (2008). MOSAIC. J. Geophys. Res. 113, D13204."
      },
      {
        "citation": "WRF v4.8.0 chem/module_mosaic_therm.F"
      }
    ]
  },
  "metaparameters": {
    "NEL": {
      "type": "integer",
      "default": 22,
      "description": "Number of MOSAIC electrolytes (nelectrolyte)."
    }
  },
  "index_sets": {
    "el": {
      "kind": "interval",
      "size": "NEL"
    },
    "solv": {
      "kind": "interval",
      "size": "NEL"
    },
    "ion": {
      "kind": "interval",
      "size": 9
    }
  },
  "models": {
    "MosaicMtemActivities": {
      "subsystems": {
        "tp": {
          "ref": "./mosaic_thermo_parameters.esm"
        }
      },
      "variables": {
        "water": {
          "type": "parameter",
          "units": "kg/m^3",
